the easiest way to make the dream of  owning a BMW a reality is to choose the  one series starting at around 23,000  euros in Germany it's served as an entry  to the BMW brand for 150,000 new buyers  making it a key model for the Bavarian  car maker tester Android sim among sites  people who complain that things were  better in the past like more 3 door  compacts with rear-wheel drive and that  those models are now a dying breed but  he says there is still a car that fits  that basic description and that's the  BMW 1-series our 118 D model with the  urban line package starts at around 34  thousand euros in Germany the 8-speed  steptronic transmission makes the 0 to  100 kilometer per hour sprint in 8.1  seconds  the 1-series has a typical BMW exterior  the headlights feature the classic twin  circular design and then there's the  unmistakable two kidney radiator grilles  Fabia Andre says it's logical in a  rear-wheel drive for the engine to be  mounted lengthwise not transverse and  take up more length of the car than  width but actually at 4.30 three meters  this compact BMW is only marginally  longer than the current VW Golf this  engine configuration leaves less room  for passengers and luggage says Andre  but his impression is that there's  plenty of room although not for a long  vacation to be exact the BMW offers 360  litres of trunk space that's just 20  litres less than the golf with  front-wheel drive a difference that most  owners won't mind  the interior is definitely up to BMW  standard even if there is a lot of  plastic here it's of high quality  workmanship and as a pleasant feel  the most noticeable effect of the  rear-wheel drive is on the right - dusty  Andre explains it like this since the  front axle doesn't exert any thrust the  steering stays free from any drive  influence so no matter how much he  accelerates out of the curve the force  exerted on the steering wheel stays the  same that makes for easier handling and  doesn't falsify the feedback he gets  from the steering many drivers of  today's mostly front-wheel drive cars  have never known this kind of handling a  large number of drivers see rear-wheel  drive as a must in sports and race cars  since it means less of a temptation to  understeer at high speeds  [Music]  named Andre points have another feature  that along with the rear-wheel-drive  emphasizes the Beamer one series is  sporting ambitions the 8-speed  steptronic transmission with most  automatics to shift manually the driver  pushes forward to shift up and pulls  back to shift down but here it's the  other way around  Andre sees that as the more logical way  to do it because that's how it works in  race cars with sequential gearboxes so  the shift lever moves in accordance with  the g-forces when accelerating and  braking so it's a logical configuration  but unfortunately it hasn't become the  norm for the great majority of cars  being visits laundry observes the BMW's  1-series occupies a niche that no other  car maker will venture into right now  that may be a very good selling point  but it might not last long  BMW makes plenty of other models that  don't have rear-wheel drive the kinda  got a problem  one harbinger of this development was  BMWs 2 Series active tourer that  utilized a transverse engine with  front-wheel drive to create more space  for the passengers this may very well be  the stars for the next generation of the  1 Series and that would spell the end of  the rear-wheel drive  era for the compact class
 

